{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I lived before a great flood.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 7:6 says Noah was six hundred years old when the flood came, so he lived before it. Enoch also lived before the flood, but this is only the first clue, and later clues rule him out."}, {"clue": "God told me to build with gopher wood.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In Genesis 6:14 God tells Noah, 'Make thee an ark of gopher wood.'"}, {"clue": "My sons were Shem, Ham and Japheth.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 6:10 says 'Noah begat three sons, Shem, Ham, and Japheth.'"}, {"clue": "I built the ark.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Hebrews 11:7 says Noah 'prepared an ark to the saving of his house.' 'Built' is a fair paraphrase."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-noah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a Nazarite from birth.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 16:17 has Samson say, \"I have been a Nazarite unto God from my mother's womb.\""}, {"clue": "I tore a young lion with my bare hands.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 14:5-6 says he \"rent him as he would have rent a kid, and he had nothing in his hand.\""}, {"clue": "Delilah learned the secret of my strength.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 16:17-18 says he \"told her all his heart\" about the razor and his strength, and Delilah saw that he had done so."}, {"clue": "I pulled down the house where the Philistines gathered.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 16:29-30 says Samson bowed on the two middle pillars and \"the house fell upon the lords, and upon all the people that were therein.\""}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-samson"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "An angel found me threshing wheat by a winepress.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 6:11-12 says Gideon threshed wheat by the winepress when the angel of the LORD appeared to him."}, {"clue": "I tested God with a fleece.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 6:36-40 describes Gideon's two fleece tests ('let me prove... with the fleece')."}, {"clue": "My army was reduced to three hundred men.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 7:7 has the LORD tell Gideon he will save Israel by the three hundred men and send the rest home, which amounts to reducing the army."}, {"clue": "We carried trumpets, pitchers and lamps.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 7:16 says Gideon ('he', in context) gave each of the three hundred a trumpet, an empty pitcher and a lamp inside it."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-gideon"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a woman of Moab.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ruth 1:4 names Ruth (and Orpah) among the women of Moab taken as wives. It also fits Orpah, but that is acceptable for an early clue."}, {"clue": "I told my mother-in-law: whither thou goest, I will go.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ruth 1:16 records Ruth saying 'whither thou goest, I will go' to Naomi, her mother-in-law."}, {"clue": "I gleaned in the fields of Boaz.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ruth 2:3 says she gleaned in a part of the field belonging to Boaz. The plural 'fields' is minor paraphrase."}, {"clue": "I became an ancestor of King David.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ruth 4:13-17: Ruth bore Obed, the father of Jesse, the father of David."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-ruth"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a prophetess.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 4:4 calls Deborah 'a prophetess.' Miriam and Huldah were also prophetesses, but this is only the first clue, so that overlap is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I judged Israel under a palm tree in mount Ephraim.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 4:5 says she dwelt under the palm tree of Deborah in mount Ephraim and that Israel came up to her for judgment. Combined with 4:4 ('she judged Israel'), this is a fair paraphrase."}, {"clue": "I summoned Barak to fight Sisera.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 4:6-7 says she sent and called Barak and relayed the LORD's command to fight against Sisera."}, {"clue": "A song of mine is recorded in Judges 5.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 5:1 says 'Then sang Deborah and Barak.' The song is attributed to her, sung jointly with Barak, so this is acceptable."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-deborah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My mother Hannah prayed for me at Shiloh.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 1:10-11 records her bitter prayer and vow for a son, but the quoted verses say only 'she'. They name neither Hannah nor Shiloh, which appear in 1:2 and 1:9."}, {"clue": "As a boy I served Eli the priest.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "The verse says 'the child Samuel ministered unto the LORD before Eli', which is service to the LORD under Eli rather than service to Eli himself. It also omits the title 'priest'; 2:11 has 'before Eli the priest'. This is close paraphrase but slightly reframed."}, {"clue": "I heard the LORD call my name in the night.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 3:10 shows the LORD calling 'Samuel, Samuel'. The night setting comes from the surrounding context in 3:3."}, {"clue": "I anointed both Saul and David.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 16:13 supports the anointing of David only. Saul's anointing is in 1 Samuel 10:1, which is not cited."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-samuel"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "As a baby I was hidden in an ark of bulrushes.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 2:3 describes the child placed in an ark of bulrushes after his mother could no longer hide him. Exodus 2:10 identifies the child as Moses."}, {"clue": "I was raised in Pharaoh's household.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 2:10 says the child was brought to Pharaoh's daughter and 'became her son,' which supports his upbringing in Pharaoh's household. Joseph served in Pharaoh's court as an adult but was not raised there."}, {"clue": "I saw a bush burn without being consumed.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 3:2 states the bush burned with fire and was not consumed, and the context identifies the one who saw it as Moses."}, {"clue": "I led Israel out of Egypt.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 7:36 says 'He brought them out' after signs in Egypt. Verse 35 makes clear this refers to Moses. Joshua led Israel into Canaan, not out of Egypt."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-moses"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was ploughing with twelve yoke of oxen when I was called.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 19:19 says Elisha was plowing with twelve yoke of oxen when Elijah cast his mantle upon him, which was his call."}, {"clue": "I asked for a double portion of my master's spirit.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 2:9 records Elisha saying, 'let a double portion of thy spirit be upon me' to Elijah."}, {"clue": "I took up the mantle that fell from Elijah.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 2:13 says he took up the mantle of Elijah that fell from him; the context identifies the subject as Elisha."}, {"clue": "I told Naaman to wash in the Jordan seven times.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 5:10 says Elisha sent the instruction by a messenger. Saying 'I told' is acceptable paraphrase."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-elisha"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was my father's favourite son.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 37:3 says Israel loved Joseph more than all his children, which matches 'favourite son.'"}, {"clue": "My brothers sold me into slavery.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Genesis 37:28 records Joseph being drawn out of the pit, sold to the Ishmeelites for twenty pieces of silver, and brought into Egypt. However, its 'they' is ambiguous in context and could refer to the Midianites. It does not explicitly name the brothers as the sellers. Genesis 37:27 (Judah: 'let us sell him') or Genesis 45:4 ('whom ye sold into Egypt') would make the brothers' role explicit."}, {"clue": "I interpreted Pharaoh's dreams.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 41:25 begins Joseph's interpretation of Pharaoh's dream to Pharaoh, which supports the clue."}, {"clue": "I was set over all the land of Egypt.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 41:41 states this verbatim."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-joseph"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a cunning hunter.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 25:27 says Esau was a cunning hunter. Nimrod was a 'mighty hunter' (Genesis 10:9), but an early clue overlapping another option is acceptable, and later clues single out Esau."}, {"clue": "I was born red and hairy.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 25:25 says the firstborn came out red, all over like an hairy garment, and was named Esau."}, {"clue": "I sold my birthright for a meal.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 25:33-34 says he sold his birthright unto Jacob and received bread and pottage of lentiles, which is fairly called a meal."}, {"clue": "I am also called Edom.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 36:1 says 'Esau, who is Edom.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-esau"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I left Ur of the Chaldees.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 11:31 says Abram went forth from Ur of the Chaldees. Lot also left Ur, but a single early clue fitting another option is acceptable."}, {"clue": "God changed my name.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 17:5 records God renaming Abram as Abraham. Jacob was also renamed (Gen 32:28), but the combined clues exclude him."}, {"clue": "My wife laughed at God's promise of a son.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 18:12 says Sarah laughed within herself about having a child in old age. The promise itself is in 18:10, but the laughter clearly responds to it, and the verse calls Abraham 'my lord'."}, {"clue": "God tested me in the land of Moriah.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 22:1-2 says God did 'tempt' (test) Abraham and sent him to the land of Moriah."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-abraham"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was born holding my twin brother's heel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 25:26 states that the second twin's hand took hold on Esau's heel and his name was called Jacob."}, {"clue": "I bought my brother's birthright with pottage.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 25:33-34 records Esau selling his birthright to Jacob, who then gave him bread and pottage of lentiles."}, {"clue": "I dreamed of a ladder reaching to heaven.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 28:12 describes the dream of a ladder whose top reached to heaven. Verse 10 of the context identifies the dreamer as Jacob."}, {"clue": "God renamed me Israel.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Genesis 32:28 records the renaming to Israel, but the speaker is 'he', the one who wrestled with Jacob. It is only implied to be God (v.30). Genesis 35:10 explicitly says 'God said unto him... Israel shall be thy name.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-jacob"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was one of twelve men sent to spy out Canaan.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Numbers 13 lists Oshea (Hoshea) the son of Nun of Ephraim among the twelve spies, and verse 16 says Moses renamed him Jehoshua (Joshua). Caleb also fits this clue, but that is acceptable for an early clue."}, {"clue": "I succeeded Moses as leader.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Joshua 1:1-2 says that after Moses' death the LORD told Joshua, Moses' minister, to lead the people over Jordan. This establishes him as Moses' successor."}, {"clue": "The walls of Jericho fell when the people shouted.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Joshua 6:20 confirms that the wall fell when the people shouted, but the verse does not name Joshua. His role (commanding the shout) is in Joshua 6:16. The clue is also not phrased in the first person, so it links to Joshua only by context."}, {"clue": "I said: As for me and my house, we will serve the LORD.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "This is a direct quotation of Joshua's words in Joshua 24:15."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-joshua"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My house was upon the town wall of Jericho.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Joshua 2:15 says her house was upon the town wall. Jericho is not named in that verse, but Joshua 2:1-3 places the account in Jericho."}, {"clue": "I hid two Israelite spies.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Joshua 2:4 says the woman took the two men and hid them. Joshua 2:1-2 identifies them as spies of the children of Israel."}, {"clue": "I let them down by a cord through a window.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Joshua 2:15 says this directly."}, {"clue": "A line of scarlet thread marked my home.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Joshua 2:18 gives only the instruction to bind the scarlet line in the window. Rahab actually binds it in 2:21, and its role as a marker comes from the context of 2:18-19, not the verse wording alone."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-rahab"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was the youngest son of Jesse.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Jesse says 'There remaineth yet the youngest' who keeps the sheep. In context this is David, named in 16:13. 1 Samuel 17:14 states it more explicitly, but the paired verse adequately supports the clue."}, {"clue": "I played the harp for King Saul.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 16:23 says David took an harp and played, and Saul was refreshed."}, {"clue": "I defeated Goliath.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 17:49-50 records David slaying the Philistine with a sling and stone. The Philistine is named Goliath in 17:4 and 17:23."}, {"clue": "I became king over Israel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 5:3 says the elders anointed David king over Israel. Saul and Solomon also became king, but the earlier clues exclude them, so the full set fits only David."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-david"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My mother was Bathsheba.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 12:24 says Bath-sheba bare a son and David called his name Solomon. Of the options, only Solomon fits; David's mother is not Bathsheba, and Rehoboam's mother was Naamah."}, {"clue": "I asked God for an understanding heart.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 3:9 records the request \"Give therefore thy servant an understanding heart.\" The context (3:5, 3:10) identifies the speaker as Solomon."}, {"clue": "I built the temple in Jerusalem.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 6:14 says \"So Solomon built the house, and finished it.\" David only prepared for it and did not build it."}, {"clue": "I judged between two women who both claimed one baby.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 3:16-27 narrates the king judging the two women's dispute over the living child. In context the king is Solomon (3:28)."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-solomon"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was of the tribe of Benjamin.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In 1 Samuel 9:21 Saul says, 'Am not I a Benjamite.' Jonathan was also a Benjamite, but this is an early clue, which is acceptable."}, {"clue": "From my shoulders upward I was higher than any of the people.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 9:2 says of Saul, 'from his shoulders and upward he was higher than any of the people.'"}, {"clue": "Samuel anointed me as Israel's first king.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 10:1 shows Samuel anointing him 'to be captain over his inheritance.' The verse does not name Saul (context does) and does not say 'first king.' That is a well-known inference, not the wording of the verse."}, {"clue": "I consulted a woman at Endor.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 28:7 shows Saul asking his servants to find a woman with a familiar spirit, and they name one at Endor. His actual consultation with her follows in 28:8 onward. This is minor."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-saul"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a Tishbite.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 17:1 explicitly says 'Elijah the Tishbite.'"}, {"clue": "Ravens fed me by the brook Cherith.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Kings 17:6 says ravens brought 'him' bread and flesh and that he drank of 'the brook', but it names neither Elijah nor Cherith. Verse 5 names the brook Cherith, so the clue is accurate, but the cited verse alone does not fully support it. Citing 1 Kings 17:5-6 would."}, {"clue": "I challenged the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 18:19-24 shows Elijah gathering the prophets of Baal to Mount Carmel and proposing the test of fire."}, {"clue": "I went up by a whirlwind into heaven.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 2:11 states 'Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-elijah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was taken captive to Babylon as a young man.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Daniel 1:3-6 says Daniel was among the 'children' of Israel brought into the king's palace, which supports his youth. The siege and the carrying away to Babylon are described in 1:1-2, so 'captive' is implied by context rather than stated in the cited verses."}, {"clue": "I purposed not to defile myself with the king's meat.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Daniel 1:8 matches this almost word for word."}, {"clue": "I read the writing on the wall.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In Daniel 5:25-28 Daniel reads and interprets MENE, MENE, TEKEL, UPHARSIN. The surrounding context identifies him as the speaker."}, {"clue": "I was delivered from the den of lions.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Daniel 6:22-23 explicitly describes his deliverance and his being taken up out of the den unhurt."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-daniel"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was cupbearer to King Artaxerxes.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Nehemiah 1:11 says 'I was the king's cupbearer' but does not name the king. Artaxerxes is named in Nehemiah 2:1, which is not cited. The clue itself is accurate."}, {"clue": "I wept over Jerusalem's broken walls.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Nehemiah 1:3-4 reports that the wall of Jerusalem was broken down, and Nehemiah says he sat down and wept."}, {"clue": "I led the rebuilding of the city wall.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Nehemiah 2:17-18 shows Nehemiah urging 'let us build up the wall of Jerusalem' and the people responding."}, {"clue": "The wall was finished in fifty-two days.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Nehemiah 6:15 states the wall was finished 'in fifty and two days.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-nehemiah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was raised by my cousin Mordecai.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Esther 2:7 says Mordecai brought up Esther, 'his uncle's daughter' (making her his cousin), and took her for his own daughter after her parents died."}, {"clue": "I became queen in Persia.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Esther 2:17 says the king set the royal crown on her head and made her queen instead of Vashti. The verse does not name Persia, though the book's setting (Esther 1:3, Shushan) makes it accurate. Vashti was also queen, but only this single early clue fits her, which is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I said: If I perish, I perish.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Esther 4:16 ends with Esther's words 'and if I perish, I perish.'"}, {"clue": "The feast of Purim remembers my people's deliverance.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Esther 9:26-28 establishes Purim as a yearly memorial kept by the Jews. The word 'deliverance' is not in these verses, but it is a fair summary of the events they commemorate (cf. Esther 9:22)."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-esther"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I saw the Lord high and lifted up in the year King Uzziah died.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Isaiah 6:1 states this directly."}, {"clue": "A seraph touched my lips with a live coal.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Isaiah 6:6-7 says a seraph laid a live coal upon his mouth and said, 'this hath touched thy lips.'"}, {"clue": "I answered: Here am I; send me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Isaiah 6:8 quotes this exactly."}, {"clue": "I prophesied of a child called Immanuel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Isaiah 7:14, spoken by Isaiah to Ahaz, foretells a son called Immanuel."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-isaiah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a prophet, the son of Amittai.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 14:25 names 'his servant Jonah, the son of Amittai, the prophet.'"}, {"clue": "I fled from God's call on a ship.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Jonah 1:3 says Jonah rose up to flee from the presence of the LORD and boarded a ship going to Tarshish."}, {"clue": "I spent three days inside a great fish.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Jonah 1:17 says Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights. The clue is an acceptable paraphrase."}, {"clue": "I was displeased when Nineveh repented.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Jonah 4:1 states Jonah's displeasure and anger. The cause (Nineveh's repentance and God sparing the city) comes from the context of Jonah 3:10, not from the verse shown."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-jonah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I lived in Nazareth of Galilee.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Gabriel was sent to Nazareth, a city of Galilee, to the virgin named Mary. This implies she lived there, and Luke 2:4 confirms it."}, {"clue": "The angel Gabriel was sent to me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:26-27 states Gabriel was sent to the virgin whose name was Mary. Gabriel appeared to Zacharias, not Elisabeth, so this does not fit another option."}, {"clue": "I was espoused to Joseph.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:27 says she was a virgin espoused to a man named Joseph."}, {"clue": "I gave birth to Jesus in Bethlehem.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Luke 2:4-7 shows Mary giving birth to her firstborn son in Bethlehem. The name Jesus is not in this passage; it appears in Luke 2:21."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-mary"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "God said He knew me before He formed me in the belly.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Jeremiah 1:5 says directly, 'Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee.'"}, {"clue": "I protested that I was only a child.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Jeremiah 1:6 has Jeremiah saying, 'I cannot speak: for I am a child.'"}, {"clue": "I was let down into a dungeon full of mire.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Jeremiah 38:6 says they 'let down Jeremiah with cords' into a dungeon where 'there was no water, but mire.' That is a fair paraphrase."}, {"clue": "Baruch wrote my words on a roll.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Jeremiah 36:4 says Baruch wrote 'from the mouth of Jeremiah' upon 'a roll of a book.' The words are the LORD's words as spoken through Jeremiah, which is acceptable paraphrase."}], "verdict": "fix", "suggested_fix": "The clues, answer and explanation are accurate, but the hint 'Look in the Major Prophets' fits all four options (Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel and Daniel are all Major Prophets in the English canon), so it gives no help in choosing. Replace it with a distinguishing hint, e.g., 'He prophesied in Judah during the days of Josiah and was imprisoned during the siege of Jerusalem,' or 'Look for the prophet whose scribe was Baruch.'", "id": "who-jeremiah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was chief among the publicans.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 19:2 says Zacchaeus 'was the chief among the publicans.'"}, {"clue": "I was rich.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 19:2 says 'and he was rich.'"}, {"clue": "I was little of stature.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 19:3 says 'because he was little of stature.'"}, {"clue": "I climbed a sycomore tree to see Jesus.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 19:4 says he 'climbed up into a sycomore tree to see him.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-zacchaeus"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My father Zacharias was a priest.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Luke 1:5 establishes that Zacharias was a priest and that his wife was Elisabeth. It does not say he was John's father. That link comes in Luke 1:13 and 1:57-63, so the cited verse alone only partly supports the clue."}, {"clue": "I wore raiment of camel's hair.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 3:4 states that John had his raiment of camel's hair. Elijah is called 'an hairy man' in 2 Kings 1:8, but camel's hair is never specified for him."}, {"clue": "I ate locusts and wild honey.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 3:4 says 'his meat was locusts and wild honey.'"}, {"clue": "I baptized Jesus in the Jordan.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Mark 1:9 states that Jesus 'was baptized of John in Jordan.' Together with the other clues, this uniquely identifies John the Baptist among the options."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-john_baptist"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a fisherman, the brother of Andrew.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 4:18 names Simon called Peter and Andrew his brother as fishers casting a net."}, {"clue": "Jesus called me to become a fisher of men.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 4:19 records Jesus saying 'I will make you fishers of men' to Peter and Andrew. It also fits Andrew, but an early clue matching another option is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I walked on the water toward Jesus.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 14:29 explicitly says Peter walked on the water to go to Jesus."}, {"clue": "I denied Jesus three times before the cock crew.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 26:75 has Peter remembering the prediction 'Before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice' and weeping bitterly. This presupposes that the denials had happened, which verses 69-74 narrate."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-peter"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was one of the twelve, called Didymus.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 20:24 calls Thomas 'one of the twelve, called Didymus.'"}, {"clue": "I was absent when Jesus first appeared to the disciples.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "John 20:24 says Thomas 'was not with them when Jesus came,' which refers to the appearance in John 20:19. The verse itself does not say 'first,' and other Gospels record earlier appearances to individual disciples (e.g., Luke 24:34). The wording is a slight overstatement but still accurate for the gathered disciples in John."}, {"clue": "I wanted to see the print of the nails.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 20:25 records Thomas saying, 'Except I shall see in his hands the print of the nails... I will not believe.'"}, {"clue": "I said: My Lord and my God.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 20:28 records Thomas saying, 'My Lord and my God.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-thomas"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a Pharisee and a ruler of the Jews.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 3:1 calls Nicodemus 'a man of the Pharisees' and 'a ruler of the Jews.'"}, {"clue": "I came to Jesus by night.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 3:2 says 'The same came to Jesus by night.' 'The same' refers to Nicodemus from verse 1."}, {"clue": "Jesus told me I must be born again.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In John 3:7 Jesus says to Nicodemus, 'Ye must be born again.' The context of John 3:1-7 identifies the addressee."}, {"clue": "I brought myrrh and aloes for Jesus' burial.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 19:39 says Nicodemus 'brought a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about an hundred pound weight.' Joseph of Arimathaea appears in the burial scene, but the text credits the spices to Nicodemus."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-nicodemus"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was born in Tarsus.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In Acts 22:3 Paul says he was 'born in Tarsus, a city in Cilicia.'"}, {"clue": "I once persecuted the church.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In Galatians 1:13 Paul writes 'beyond measure I persecuted the church of God, and wasted it.'"}, {"clue": "A light from heaven blinded me near Damascus.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 9:3-8 describes a light from heaven near Damascus. When his eyes were opened 'he saw no man' and he was led by the hand, which implies blindness. The passage does not explicitly say the light caused it. Acts 22:11 ('I could not see for the glory of that light') would make the link explicit."}, {"clue": "I wrote many letters to the churches.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "2 Peter 3:15-16 refers to 'all his epistles,' which supports multiple letters. The words 'many' and 'to the churches' are mild inferences rather than explicit wording."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-paul"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was one of seven chosen to serve the church.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 6:5 names seven chosen men, including Stephen, but does not state the number or the serving role. Those come from Acts 6:2-3 (\"seven men… whom we may appoint over this business\" of serving tables). Philip also fits this clue, which is acceptable for an early clue."}, {"clue": "I was full of faith and power.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 6:8 says \"Stephen, full of faith and power.\""}, {"clue": "As I spoke I saw the heavens opened.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 7:56 has Stephen saying \"Behold, I see the heavens opened.\""}, {"clue": "I was stoned while a young man named Saul watched.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 7:58 says he was stoned and the witnesses laid their clothes at the feet of a young man named Saul. \"Watched\" is a reasonable paraphrase, and Acts 8:1 adds that Saul was consenting."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-stephen"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I lived in Bethany with my sister and brother.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "John 11:1 names Bethany as the town of Mary and her sister Martha and says Lazarus was of Bethany. It does not say Lazarus was their brother; that comes in John 11:2 ('whose brother Lazarus was sick'). Living 'with' them in one household is also inferred rather than stated."}, {"clue": "I was cumbered about much serving.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 10:40 states this of Martha almost verbatim."}, {"clue": "Jesus said my sister had chosen the good part.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 10:42 says Mary has chosen 'that good part', and Mary is Martha's sister (Luke 10:39). Jesus is speaking to Martha (Luke 10:41)."}, {"clue": "I welcomed Jesus into my house.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 10:38 says Martha 'received him into her house.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-martha"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My name is interpreted 'the son of consolation'.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 4:36 states that Joses was surnamed Barnabas, 'which is, being interpreted, The son of consolation.'"}, {"clue": "I sold land and laid the money at the apostles' feet.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 4:37, which continues the sentence about Barnabas in 4:36, says he sold land and laid the money at the apostles' feet."}, {"clue": "I brought Saul to the apostles in Jerusalem.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 9:27 says Barnabas brought Saul to the apostles. The Jerusalem setting comes from Acts 9:26 and is not in the quoted verse, but it is accurate from context."}, {"clue": "I travelled with Paul on his first missionary journey.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 13:2-4 shows Barnabas and Saul sent out together. 'First missionary journey' is a later scholarly label, and the text calls him Saul. Mark also went on this journey (Acts 13:5), but the earlier clues rule him out."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-barnabas"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I lived in Bethany.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 11:1 names 'Lazarus, of Bethany.' Simon the leper was also of Bethany, but this is only an early clue."}, {"clue": "My sisters were Mary and Martha.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 11:1-2 calls Martha Mary's sister and calls Lazarus Mary's brother, so Martha is clearly his sister too (stated outright in John 11:39)."}, {"clue": "Jesus wept near my grave.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "John 11:35 says only 'Jesus wept.' The setting near the grave comes from the context (vv. 34, 38), not the cited verse. In fact v.38 has Jesus coming to the grave after weeping, so 'near' is a slight stretch."}, {"clue": "Jesus called me out of the tomb.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 11:43-44: 'Lazarus, come forth. And he that was dead came forth.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-lazarus"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a seller of purple.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:14 calls Lydia 'a seller of purple.'"}, {"clue": "I came from the city of Thyatira.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:14 says Lydia was 'of the city of Thyatira.'"}, {"clue": "I heard Paul speak by a riverside at Philippi.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 16:13-14 supports hearing Paul 'by a river side' outside the city. The city is named as Philippi only in 16:12, which is not in the shown text. The clue is accurate but goes slightly beyond the cited passage."}, {"clue": "My household was baptized.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:15: 'when she was baptized, and her household.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-lydia"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a tiller of the ground.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 4:2 states 'Cain was a tiller of the ground,' while Abel was a keeper of sheep."}, {"clue": "God did not respect my offering.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 4:5 says 'unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect.'"}, {"clue": "I asked: Am I my brother's keeper?", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 4:9 records Cain saying, 'I know not: Am I my brother's keeper?'"}, {"clue": "God set a mark upon me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 4:15 says 'the LORD set a mark upon Cain.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-cain"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I walked with God.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 5:22 states that Enoch walked with God. Noah also walked with God (Genesis 6:9), but this is an early clue, and the later clues narrow the answer."}, {"clue": "I was the father of Methuselah.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 5:21 states that Enoch begat Methuselah."}, {"clue": "I was not, for God took me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 5:24 quotes this almost word for word."}, {"clue": "Hebrews says I was translated so I should not see death.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Hebrews 11:5 states that Enoch was translated that he should not see death."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-enoch"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was formed of the dust of the ground.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 2:7 says the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground. In context this man is the one called Adam in 2:19-20."}, {"clue": "I gave names to the animals.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 2:20 states that Adam gave names to all cattle, fowl, and beasts of the field."}, {"clue": "I lived in the garden of Eden.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 2:15 says the LORD God put the man into the garden of Eden to dress it and keep it."}, {"clue": "My wife was Eve.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 3:20 says Adam called his wife's name Eve."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-adam"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My grandmother Lois and mother Eunice shared unfeigned faith.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Timothy 1:5 says the unfeigned faith dwelt first in his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice, and also in Timothy."}, {"clue": "I was Paul's young companion.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Timothy 4:12 ('Let no man despise thy youth') supports his youth. The cited text does not show him travelling with Paul, though Acts 16:1-3 does."}, {"clue": "Paul called me his dearly beloved son.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Timothy 1:2 reads 'To Timothy, my dearly beloved son.'"}, {"clue": "Two New Testament letters are addressed to me.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Timothy 1:2 shows only one letter addressed to Timothy. The second (2 Timothy 1:2) is true but is not part of this clue's reference. Titus has only one letter, so the clue still points uniquely to Timothy."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-timothy"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My father was Enoch.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 5:21 states Enoch begat Methuselah."}, {"clue": "My son was Lamech.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 5:25 states Methuselah begat Lamech."}, {"clue": "I was Noah's grandfather.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Genesis 5:28-29 shows Lamech fathered Noah but does not name Methuselah. The grandfather link only follows when combined with 5:25, which is cited for the previous clue."}, {"clue": "I lived nine hundred sixty and nine years.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 5:27 states this exactly."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-methuselah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I lived in the land of Uz.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Job 1:1 places Job in the land of Uz. The friends are identified as a Temanite, a Shuhite and a Buzite, not as men of Uz."}, {"clue": "I was perfect and upright.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Job 1:1 says Job was 'perfect and upright'."}, {"clue": "I lost my children and possessions in a single day.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Job 1:13-19 describes 'a day' on which messengers arrived one after another ('while he was yet speaking'). They reported the loss of oxen, asses, sheep, camels, servants, and the sons and daughters who died when the house fell."}, {"clue": "I said: The LORD gave, and the LORD hath taken away.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Job 1:21 quotes Job saying this."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-job"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was king of Salem.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 14:18 explicitly calls Melchizedek 'king of Salem.'"}, {"clue": "I was priest of the most high God.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 14:18 says 'he was the priest of the most high God.'"}, {"clue": "I brought forth bread and wine to Abram.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Genesis 14:18 says he 'brought forth bread and wine' but does not name Abram in that verse. That he brought it to Abram comes from the surrounding context (14:17-20), so the clue is accurate in substance."}, {"clue": "Abram gave me tithes of all.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Hebrews 7:2 says 'Abraham gave a tenth part of all' to Melchizedek, which matches the clue. Genesis 14:20 ('he gave him tithes of all') matches the wording even more closely."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-melchizedek"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was captain of the host of the king of Syria.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 5:1 calls Naaman 'captain of the host of the king of Syria.'"}, {"clue": "I was a leper.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 5:1 ends 'but he was a leper.' Gehazi later became leprous (5:27), but the earlier clue already rules him out."}, {"clue": "A captive little maid from Israel told of a prophet.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 5:2-3 says a captive little maid who served Naaman's wife spoke of the prophet in Samaria who could heal him."}, {"clue": "I dipped myself seven times in the Jordan.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 5:14 says he 'dipped himself seven times in Jordan.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-naaman"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was one of the twelve spies.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Numbers 13:6 names Caleb son of Jephunneh of Judah in the list of men sent to search the land. Joshua also fits this clue, but that is acceptable for an early clue."}, {"clue": "I brought back a good report with Joshua.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Numbers 14:6-9 shows Caleb and Joshua together telling the people the land is exceeding good and urging them not to fear."}, {"clue": "I was eighty-five years old when I asked for my inheritance.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Joshua 14:10 has Caleb say 'I am this day fourscore and five years old' while requesting his inheritance."}, {"clue": "I asked for the mountain and received Hebron.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Joshua 14:12-13 records Caleb saying 'give me this mountain' and Joshua giving him Hebron for an inheritance."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-caleb"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My husband was Elkanah.", "supported": "no", "reason": "1 Samuel 1:2 says only 'he had two wives... Hannah... Peninnah.' The shown text never names Elkanah; his name is in 1 Samuel 1:1. The clue's key fact is therefore not in the displayed Scripture. It also fits Peninnah, which is acceptable for an early clue."}, {"clue": "I prayed silently at Shiloh, and Eli thought I was drunk.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1:13 supports the silent prayer (lips moved, voice not heard) and Eli thinking she was drunken. Shiloh is not in 1:13; it appears in 1:9."}, {"clue": "I vowed to give my son to the LORD.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1:11: 'she vowed a vow... then I will give him unto the LORD all the days of his life.'"}, {"clue": "I named my son Samuel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1:20: 'she bare a son, and called his name Samuel.' This uniquely identifies Hannah among the options."}], "verdict": "fix", "suggested_fix": "Change clue 1's reference to 1 Samuel 1:1-2 and display both verses, so the text shows Elkanah as husband of Hannah and Peninnah. Optionally change clue 2's reference to 1 Samuel 1:9-13 so Shiloh is supported, or drop 'at Shiloh' from the clue. The hint, answer, and explanation (1:20 falls within 1:13-20) are fine.", "id": "who-hannah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "Seven devils had gone out of me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 8:2 says of 'Mary called Magdalene, out of whom went seven devils.'"}, {"clue": "I stood by the cross.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 19:25 lists Mary Magdalene among those who 'stood by the cross of Jesus.'"}, {"clue": "I came to the tomb while it was yet dark.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 20:1 says Mary Magdalene came 'early, when it was yet dark, unto the sepulchre.'"}, {"clue": "At first I supposed the risen Jesus was the gardener.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 20:15 says 'She, supposing him to be the gardener.' The context (John 20:11, 16) identifies her as Mary, and the explanation's reference includes 20:16."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-mary_magdalene"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a publican.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 10:3 explicitly calls him 'Matthew the publican.'"}, {"clue": "I sat at the receipt of custom.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 9:9 says Jesus saw Matthew 'sitting at the receipt of custom.'"}, {"clue": "Jesus said to me: Follow me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 9:9: 'he saith unto him, Follow me.'"}, {"clue": "Jesus ate with publicans and sinners at my table.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Luke 5:29-30 names Levi, not Matthew. Equating Levi with Matthew is an inference from the parallel accounts (Matthew 9:9-10; Mark 2:14-15), not something this text states. Matthew 9:10 itself says only 'in the house' without naming whose. Players shown only Luke's text may be confused, and the clue also loosely echoes Zacchaeus (Luke 19:5-7)."}], "verdict": "fix", "suggested_fix": "Change clue 4's reference to Matthew 9:9-11, which names Matthew and records Jesus sitting at meat with 'many publicans and sinners' and the Pharisees' objection. Reword the clue to 'Jesus sat at meat with publicans and sinners in the house after calling me.' Alternatively, keep Luke 5:29-30 but add a note that Luke calls this publican Levi, whom the parallel accounts (Matthew 9:9; Mark 2:14) identify with the one called from the receipt of custom.", "id": "who-matthew"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was one of the twelve.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 26:14 explicitly calls Judas Iscariot 'one of the twelve.' This early clue fits every option, which is acceptable because clues are revealed one at a time."}, {"clue": "I had the bag.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "John 12:6 says 'he ... had the bag,' but the displayed verse never names Judas. His identity comes from John 12:4, which is not shown. Citing John 12:4-6 would make the support self-contained."}, {"clue": "I agreed to betray Jesus for thirty pieces of silver.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 26:15: 'they covenanted with him for thirty pieces of silver,' with Judas named in the preceding verse."}, {"clue": "I betrayed Jesus with a kiss.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 26:48-49 describes 'he that betrayed him' giving the kiss as a sign and then kissing Jesus. Verse 47 names this person as Judas."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-judas"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a son of Zebedee.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 4:21 names James and John as sons of Zebedee. It also fits the James option, but this is an early clue and later clues narrow the answer."}, {"clue": "Jesus surnamed my brother and me Boanerges.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Mark 3:17 says James and John were surnamed Boanerges. This clue also fits James, but that is acceptable for a non-final clue."}, {"clue": "Tradition identifies me as the disciple whom Jesus loved.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "John 21:20 mentions the disciple whom Jesus loved but does not name him. The clue openly frames the identification as tradition, so it is not misleading, though the text itself does not establish it."}, {"clue": "I was on the isle of Patmos for the word of God.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Revelation 1:9 has 'I John' on Patmos for the word of God. Equating this John with the apostle is traditional rather than stated, which the explanation acknowledges. It excludes James, Andrew and Philip, so the full clue set points uniquely to John."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-john_apostle"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was the first woman.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 2:22 records God making a woman from the man's rib, the first woman created; Genesis 3:20 identifies her as Eve."}, {"clue": "I was made from a rib taken from the man.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 2:22 states the rib taken from man was made into a woman."}, {"clue": "The serpent spoke to me in the garden.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 3:1 says the serpent 'said unto the woman' and refers to the trees of the garden."}, {"clue": "I am called the mother of all living.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 3:20 states Adam named her Eve because she was the mother of all living."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-eve"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I watched over my baby brother at the river.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Exodus 2:4 says 'his sister stood afar off, to wit what would be done to him,' which fits watching over him. However, the verse does not name Miriam, and the river setting comes from verse 3. Identifying this sister as Miriam rests on Numbers 26:59, not on this verse."}, {"clue": "I am called a prophetess.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 15:20 says 'Miriam the prophetess.' Deborah is also called a prophetess (Judges 4:4), but an early single clue fitting another option is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I led the women with a timbrel after the Red Sea crossing.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 15:20 says Miriam took a timbrel and 'all the women went out after her.' The Red Sea setting is clear from the immediate context (Exodus 15:19)."}, {"clue": "I was briefly struck with leprosy.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Numbers 12:10 confirms Miriam became leprous. 'Briefly' is not in this verse; the seven days of being shut out are in Numbers 12:14-15."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-miriam"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a mighty man of wealth in Bethlehem.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Ruth 2:1 calls Boaz 'a mighty man of wealth' but does not mention Bethlehem. His link to Bethlehem appears in Ruth 2:4 ('Boaz came from Bethlehem'), which is not cited."}, {"clue": "I let a Moabite woman glean in my fields.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Ruth 2:8 has Boaz telling Ruth to glean in his field and stay there. The verse does not call her a Moabite, though Ruth 2:2 and 2:6 do."}, {"clue": "I redeemed the inheritance of Elimelech.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ruth 4:9-10 has Boaz say he has bought all that was Elimelech's to raise up the name of the dead upon his inheritance."}, {"clue": "I married Ruth.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ruth 4:13 says 'So Boaz took Ruth, and she was his wife.' Mahlon, her earlier husband, is not among the options."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-boaz"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I drew water for a stranger's camels at a well.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 24:19-20 shows her offering to draw water and drawing for all his camels. Context (24:15-18) identifies her as Rebekah and the man as Abraham's servant, a stranger to her."}, {"clue": "I was the sister of Laban.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 24:29: 'Rebekah had a brother, and his name was Laban.' Rachel and Leah were Laban's daughters, not his sisters."}, {"clue": "I married Isaac.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 24:67: Isaac 'took Rebekah, and she became his wife.'"}, {"clue": "I helped my son Jacob receive his father's blessing.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 27:6-17 shows Rebekah instructing Jacob, preparing the meat, and dressing him in Esau's raiment and the goat skins so he could receive Isaac's blessing."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-rebekah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My father was Laban.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 29:16 names Rachel as Laban's younger daughter. Leah also fits this clue, but it is an early clue and later clues narrow the answer."}, {"clue": "Jacob served seven years for me, and they seemed but a few days.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 29:20 says this almost word for word."}, {"clue": "I was the mother of Joseph and Benjamin.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 35:24 lists Joseph and Benjamin as the sons of Rachel."}, {"clue": "I died on the way to Bethlehem.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 35:19 says Rachel died and was buried 'in the way to Ephrath, which is Beth-lehem.' The clue is a fair paraphrase."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-rachel"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was Abram's nephew.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 12:5 calls Lot Abram's \"brother's son\", which makes him Abram's nephew."}, {"clue": "I chose all the plain of Jordan.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 13:11 says \"Lot chose him all the plain of Jordan.\""}, {"clue": "Two angels came to my house in Sodom.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 19:1-3 says two angels came to Sodom and entered Lot's house at his urging."}, {"clue": "My wife looked back and became a pillar of salt.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In context, Genesis 19:26 refers to Lot's wife looking back and becoming a pillar of salt."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-lot"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My mother laughed when my birth was promised.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 18:12 records Sarah laughing at the promise of a son, made in 18:10. Sarah is Isaac's mother (Genesis 21:3). The verse does not name Isaac, but the context makes the link clear."}, {"clue": "My father was a hundred years old when I was born.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 21:5 states directly that Abraham was a hundred years old when Isaac was born."}, {"clue": "I was laid on an altar in the land of Moriah.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Genesis 22:9 confirms Isaac was bound and laid on the altar, but calls the site only 'the place which God had told him of'. 'The land of Moriah' is named in Genesis 22:2, not in the cited verse."}, {"clue": "My sons were Esau and Jacob.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Genesis 25:25-26 names Esau and Jacob as the twins and says Isaac was threescore years old when they were born. Verses 19-21 make clear they are Isaac's sons."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-isaac"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a Levite and the brother of Moses.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 4:14 calls him 'Aaron the Levite thy brother', spoken to Moses."}, {"clue": "I spoke to Pharaoh on Moses' behalf.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 7:2 says 'Aaron thy brother shall speak unto Pharaoh.'"}, {"clue": "I made a golden calf when the people asked.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 32:1-4 says the people asked Aaron to make gods, and he took their golden earrings and made a molten calf, so it was golden."}, {"clue": "My rod budded and yielded almonds.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Numbers 17:8 says the rod of Aaron budded, bloomed blossoms and yielded almonds."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-aaron"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I am called a soothsayer.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Joshua 13:22 explicitly calls Balaam the son of Beor 'the soothsayer.'"}, {"clue": "Balak, king of Moab, sent for me to curse Israel.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Numbers 22:5-6 shows messengers sent to Balaam asking him to curse the people. However, the quoted text says only 'He sent messengers.' Balak's name and his title as king of Moab come from verse 4, which is not shown. The clue is accurate, but the displayed text does not fully establish it."}, {"clue": "An angel with a drawn sword stood in my way.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Numbers 22:31 says Balaam saw the angel of the LORD standing in the way with his sword drawn in his hand."}, {"clue": "My ass spoke to me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Numbers 22:28 says the LORD opened the mouth of the ass and she spoke to Balaam."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-balaam"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a priest at Shiloh.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 1:9 places the scene in Shiloh and calls him 'Eli the priest' sitting by the temple of the LORD. Samuel also served at Shiloh, but an early clue fitting another option is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I thought Hannah was drunken as she prayed.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 1:13-14 states directly that 'Eli thought she had been drunken.'"}, {"clue": "My sons Hophni and Phinehas were wicked.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 2:12 says Eli's sons were 'sons of Belial' and 'knew not the LORD,' which supports wickedness. However, the verse does not name Hophni and Phinehas; the names come from 1 Samuel 1:3 and 4:11."}, {"clue": "I fell backward and died when I heard the ark was taken.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 4:18 records that he fell backward and died when the ark of God was mentioned. That the ark was 'taken' is stated in 4:17, not in the cited verse."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-eli"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was the son of King Saul.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 14:1 calls him 'Jonathan the son of Saul.' Ishbosheth was also Saul's son, but a single early clue fitting another option is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I climbed up to a Philistine garrison with my armourbearer.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 14:13 says Jonathan climbed up on his hands and feet with his armourbearer after him. The garrison context comes from 14:1 and 14:11-12."}, {"clue": "My soul was knit with the soul of David.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 18:1 matches nearly verbatim."}, {"clue": "I warned David by shooting arrows.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 20:36-37 shows Jonathan shooting an arrow beyond the lad and calling out. That this was a prearranged warning to David is established in 1 Samuel 20:20-22 rather than in the cited verses, but the clue is accurate in context."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-jonathan"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was the wife of Uriah the Hittite.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 11:3 identifies Bath-sheba as 'the wife of Uriah the Hittite.'"}, {"clue": "King David saw me washing as he walked on his roof.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 11:2 says David walked upon the roof of the king's house and saw a woman washing herself. Verse 3 identifies her as Bath-sheba."}, {"clue": "I became the mother of Solomon.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 12:24 says Bath-sheba bore a son and he called his name Solomon."}, {"clue": "My son King Solomon set a seat for me at his right hand.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 2:19 says Solomon caused a seat to be set for the king's mother, and she sat on his right hand."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-bathsheba"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a priest of Midian.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 3:1 calls Jethro 'the priest of Midian.'"}, {"clue": "I was Moses' father-in-law.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 18:1 names Jethro as 'Moses' father in law.'"}, {"clue": "Moses kept my flock near Horeb.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Exodus 3:1 says Moses kept Jethro's flock and led it to Horeb, the mountain of God. 'Near Horeb' is a fair paraphrase."}, {"clue": "I advised Moses to appoint rulers to judge the people.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Verses 21-22 contain the advice about rulers judging the people, but the quoted text does not name the speaker. Verse 17 identifies him as Moses' father in law. Citing Exodus 18:17-22 would make the attribution explicit."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-jethro"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a champion of the Philistines.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 17:4 says a champion went out of the camp of the Philistines, named Goliath."}, {"clue": "I came from Gath.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 17:4 calls him 'Goliath, of Gath.' Achish was also of Gath, but this is acceptable for a single early clue."}, {"clue": "My height was six cubits and a span.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 17:4 gives his height as 'six cubits and a span.'"}, {"clue": "A shepherd boy struck me down with a sling and a stone.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "17:49-50 confirms that David slew the Philistine with a sling and a stone. The cited verses do not call David a shepherd boy; that detail comes from elsewhere in the chapter (17:15, 17:33-34, 17:42)."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-goliath"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a son of King David.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 3:3 lists Absalom, son of Maacah, as David's third son born in Hebron (context 3:2). This clue alone also fits the other options, which is acceptable for an opening clue."}, {"clue": "I stole the hearts of the men of Israel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 15:6 says this almost verbatim: 'so Absalom stole the hearts of the men of Israel.'"}, {"clue": "I led a rebellion against my father.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 15:10-12 describes Absalom proclaiming himself king in Hebron, drawing away David's counsellor Ahithophel, and a growing 'conspiracy' against David."}, {"clue": "My head caught in an oak as my mule went on.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 18:9 says his head caught hold of the oak and the mule under him went away."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-absalom"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a king of Israel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 16:29 states Ahab the son of Omri reigned over Israel in Samaria. The clue also fits Jeroboam, Omri and Jehu, but it is an early clue, which is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I married Jezebel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 16:31 says he took to wife Jezebel the daughter of Ethbaal. In context (v.30), 'he' is Ahab, and this uniquely identifies him among the options."}, {"clue": "I asked Elijah, Art thou he that troubleth Israel?", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 18:17 quotes Ahab's words to Elijah exactly."}, {"clue": "I wanted Naboth's vineyard for a garden of herbs.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 21:2 records Ahab asking Naboth for his vineyard 'for a garden of herbs.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-ahab"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a king of Judah.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 18:1 says Hezekiah son of Ahaz king of Judah began to reign. The clue is generic, but it is an opening clue, so fitting every option is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I broke in pieces the brasen serpent that Moses had made.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 18:4 states he brake in pieces the brasen serpent that Moses had made. This is unique to Hezekiah among the options."}, {"clue": "Isaiah told me to set my house in order.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Isaiah 38:1 records Isaiah telling the sick Hezekiah, 'Set thine house in order.'"}, {"clue": "God added fifteen years to my life.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Isaiah 38:5 says 'I will add unto thy days fifteen years,' addressed to Hezekiah."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-hezekiah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a prophet in David's court.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 7:2 calls him 'Nathan the prophet', and the king speaks with him directly, which reasonably supports a prophet at David's court. Gad also fits this clue, but it is an early clue, so that is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I told David his son would build God's house.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Verses 12-13 contain the promise that David's seed 'shall build an house for my name'. However, the quoted text does not show that Nathan is the one delivering it; that comes from 7:4-5 and 7:17. 'Son' is a fair reading of 'thy seed... out of thy bowels'."}, {"clue": "I told David a parable about a poor man's ewe lamb.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 12:1-4 explicitly says the LORD sent Nathan to David, and Nathan tells the parable of the poor man's one little ewe lamb."}, {"clue": "I said to David, Thou art the man.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Samuel 12:7 quotes this exactly."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-nathan"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was the daughter of the king of the Zidonians.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 16:31 calls Jezebel 'the daughter of Ethbaal king of the Zidonians.'"}, {"clue": "I was the wife of King Ahab.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 16:31 says 'he took to wife Jezebel'. The verse itself does not name Ahab, but in context (16:29-30) 'he' is Ahab, and 1 Kings 21:7 calls her 'Jezebel his wife.'"}, {"clue": "I threatened Elijah's life after Mount Carmel.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 19:1-2 records that after Ahab told her what Elijah had done, Jezebel sent a messenger vowing to make Elijah's life as the life of the slain prophets."}, {"clue": "I arranged Naboth's death to take his vineyard.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Kings 21:7-15 shows Jezebel writing letters in Ahab's name to have Naboth falsely accused and stoned. She then told Ahab to take possession of the vineyard."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-jezebel"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I became king of Judah at eight years old.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 22:1 states Josiah was eight years old when he began to reign in Jerusalem. Joash was seven (2 Kings 11:21), Hezekiah twenty-five, and Jehoiakim twenty-five, so no distractor fits."}, {"clue": "The book of the law was found in the temple in my reign.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 22:8 records Hilkiah finding the book of the law in the house of the LORD. The context (22:3) places this in the eighteenth year of king Josiah."}, {"clue": "I rent my clothes when it was read to me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 22:11 says the king rent his clothes when he heard the words of the book. 22:10 confirms Shaphan read it before the king."}, {"clue": "I kept a great passover.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "2 Kings 23:21-23 describes Josiah's unparalleled passover. Hezekiah also kept a great passover (2 Chronicles 30), but the earlier clues exclude him, and clues revealed one at a time may individually overlap."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-josiah"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a priest among the captives by the river Chebar.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Ezekiel 1:3 names Ezekiel the priest in the land of the Chaldeans by the river Chebar. The phrase 'among the captives' comes from Ezekiel 1:1, not 1:3, though exile is implied by 'land of the Chaldeans.'"}, {"clue": "I saw wheels whose rings were full of eyes.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ezekiel 1:18 says the rings were full of eyes. The context (1:15-16) shows these are the rings of the wheels Ezekiel saw."}, {"clue": "I lay on my side for many days as a sign.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ezekiel 4:4-6 commands Ezekiel to lie on his left side 390 days and on his right side 40 days, bearing Israel's and Judah's iniquity as a sign."}, {"clue": "I prophesied to a valley of dry bones.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ezekiel 37:4 commands him to prophesy to the dry bones. The valley setting is in 37:1, and minor paraphrase is fine."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-ezekiel"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a priest of the course of Abia.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:5 names Zacharias as a priest of the course of Abia."}, {"clue": "An angel appeared to me by the altar of incense.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:11 says an angel appeared to him standing on the right side of the altar of incense."}, {"clue": "I was struck dumb because I did not believe.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:20 says he would be dumb because he believed not the angel's words."}, {"clue": "I wrote: His name is John.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:63 records him writing 'His name is John.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-zacharias"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a priest and a ready scribe in the law of Moses.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Ezra 7:6 supports 'a ready scribe in the law of Moses' but does not call Ezra a priest. That is stated in Ezra 7:11 ('Ezra the priest, the scribe') and implied by the Aaronic genealogy in 7:1-5."}, {"clue": "I went up from Babylon in the reign of Artaxerxes.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Ezra 7:1 and 7:6 state that in the reign of Artaxerxes, Ezra went up from Babylon."}, {"clue": "I read the book of the law to the people.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Nehemiah 8:2-3 says Ezra the priest brought the law before the congregation and read therein."}, {"clue": "I stood upon a pulpit of wood.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Nehemiah 8:4 says 'Ezra the scribe stood upon a pulpit of wood.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-ezra"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was among the herdmen of Tekoa.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Amos 1:1 says Amos 'was among the herdmen of Tekoa.'"}, {"clue": "I was a gatherer of sycomore fruit.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Amos 7:14 has Amos saying 'I was an herdman, and a gatherer of sycomore fruit.'"}, {"clue": "I said I was no prophet, neither a prophet's son.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Amos 7:14 has Amos telling Amaziah 'I was no prophet, neither was I a prophet's son.'"}, {"clue": "I cried: let judgment run down as waters.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Amos 5:24 is part of the LORD's oracle delivered through Amos. Attributing the line to Amos as the prophet who spoke it is acceptable, but 'I cried' adds framing the verse does not contain."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-amos"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was of the daughters of Aaron.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:5 states Zacharias's wife was of the daughters of Aaron, and her name was Elisabeth."}, {"clue": "I was barren and well stricken in years.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:7 states Elisabeth was barren and that they were both well stricken in years."}, {"clue": "My babe leaped in my womb when Mary greeted me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 1:41 says that when Elisabeth heard Mary's salutation, the babe leaped in her womb."}, {"clue": "I was the mother of John the Baptist.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Luke 1:57-60 shows Elisabeth bore a son and insisted he be called John. The passage itself does not use the title 'the Baptist', although this John is the Baptist (Luke 3)."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-elisabeth"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a just and devout man in Jerusalem.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:25 states Simeon was a man in Jerusalem who was just and devout."}, {"clue": "The Holy Ghost revealed I would not die before seeing the Lord's Christ.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:26 says it was revealed by the Holy Ghost that he should not see death before seeing the Lord's Christ."}, {"clue": "I took the child Jesus up in my arms in the temple.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:27-28 says he came into the temple and took the child Jesus up in his arms."}, {"clue": "I said: Lord, now lettest thou thy servant depart in peace.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:29 quotes this exactly."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-simeon"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a prophet to Israel.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Hosea 1:1 says the word of the LORD came to Hosea in the days of Jeroboam king of Israel, which implies a ministry to Israel but does not explicitly call him a prophet to Israel. As an early clue it also fits Amos, which is acceptable."}, {"clue": "I obeyed God and took Gomer as my wife.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Hosea 1:3 says 'So he went and took Gomer the daughter of Diblaim,' which is his response to God's command in 1:2. This is a fair paraphrase."}, {"clue": "I named a son Loammi.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Hosea 1:9 records God's command 'Call his name Lo-ammi.' The shown verse does not state that Hosea did the naming, nor that the child was a son (that is in 1:8). The meaning is implied and minor."}, {"clue": "I bought a woman for fifteen pieces of silver and barley.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Hosea 3:2 says 'I bought her to me for fifteen pieces of silver, and for an homer of barley, and an half homer of barley.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-hosea"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was from Cana of Galilee.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 21:2 names \"Nathanael of Cana in Galilee.\""}, {"clue": "I asked: Can there any good thing come out of Nazareth?", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:46 records Nathanael asking exactly this question."}, {"clue": "Jesus saw me under the fig tree.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:48 has Jesus say, \"when thou wast under the fig tree, I saw thee.\""}, {"clue": "Jesus called me an Israelite indeed, in whom is no guile.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:47 quotes Jesus saying this of Nathanael."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-nathanael"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was from Bethsaida.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:44 states Philip was of Bethsaida. Andrew was also from there, but an early clue fitting another option is acceptable."}, {"clue": "Jesus said to me, Follow me.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:43 records Jesus finding Philip and saying, Follow me."}, {"clue": "I told Nathanael: Come and see.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:46 records Philip answering Nathanael, Come and see."}, {"clue": "I asked Jesus: Lord, shew us the Father.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 14:8 quotes Philip saying, Lord, shew us the Father. This clue uniquely identifies Philip."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-philip_apostle"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was of the house and lineage of David.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:4 states Joseph 'was of the house and lineage of David.'"}, {"clue": "An angel told me in a dream not to fear to take Mary as my wife.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 1:20: the angel appeared to Joseph in a dream and said, 'fear not to take unto thee Mary thy wife.'"}, {"clue": "I took the young child and his mother into Egypt by night.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 2:14 says he took the young child and his mother by night and departed into Egypt."}, {"clue": "People called Jesus the carpenter's son.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 13:55 records the people asking, 'Is not this the carpenter's son? is not his mother called Mary?' This identifies Mary's husband as the carpenter."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-joseph_husband"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a prophetess of the tribe of Aser.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:36 calls Anna 'a prophetess... of the tribe of Aser.'"}, {"clue": "I was a widow of about fourscore and four years.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:37 says 'she was a widow of about fourscore and four years.'"}, {"clue": "I served God with fastings and prayers night and day.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 2:37 says she 'served God with fastings and prayers night and day.'"}, {"clue": "I gave thanks when the child Jesus was brought to the temple.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Luke 2:38 says she 'coming in that instant gave thanks likewise unto the Lord' and spoke of him. The detail that the child Jesus was brought to the temple comes from the surrounding context (Luke 2:22, 27), not from verse 38 itself. This is an acceptable contextual paraphrase."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-anna"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was the Roman governor of Judaea.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Luke 3:1 explicitly says 'Pontius Pilate being governor of Judæa.' Felix also governed Judaea, but an early clue fitting another option is acceptable, and the later clues exclude him."}, {"clue": "My wife sent word about a dream concerning a just man.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 27:19 says his wife warned him about 'that just man' because of a dream. In context (vv. 17, 22) 'he' is Pilate."}, {"clue": "I asked: What is truth?", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 18:38 says 'Pilate saith unto him, What is truth?'"}, {"clue": "I washed my hands before the multitude.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 27:24 says Pilate 'washed his hands before the multitude.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-pilate"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was from Bethsaida.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:44 calls Bethsaida 'the city of Andrew and Peter.' Philip also fits this clue, but that is acceptable for an early clue, and later clues exclude him."}, {"clue": "I was one of two who heard John the Baptist speak and followed Jesus.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:40 states that one of the two who heard John speak and followed him was Andrew."}, {"clue": "I brought my brother Simon to Jesus.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 1:41-42 says he found his own brother Simon and 'brought him to Jesus.'"}, {"clue": "I told Jesus of a lad with five barley loaves.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In John 6:8-9, Andrew tells Jesus there is a lad with five barley loaves and two small fishes."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-andrew"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I lived in Joppa.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 9:36 says there was at Joppa a certain disciple named Tabitha (Dorcas)."}, {"clue": "I was full of good works and almsdeeds.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 9:36 says she was full of good works and almsdeeds which she did."}, {"clue": "I made coats and garments that the widows showed.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 9:39 says the widows stood by Peter, shewing the coats and garments which Dorcas made."}, {"clue": "Peter said to me, arise, and I opened my eyes.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 9:40 says Peter said, 'Tabitha, arise,' and she opened her eyes."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-dorcas"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was high priest that same year.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 11:49 names Caiaphas as 'the high priest that same year.'"}, {"clue": "I said it was expedient that one man should die for the people.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 11:50 continues Caiaphas's speech from 11:49: 'it is expedient for us, that one man should die for the people.' The speaker is named in 11:49, and John 18:14 confirms it."}, {"clue": "Annas was my father-in-law.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 18:13: Annas 'was father in law to Caiaphas.'"}, {"clue": "Annas sent Jesus to me bound.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "John 18:24: 'Annas had sent him bound unto Caiaphas the high priest.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-caiaphas"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a Pharisee and a doctor of the law.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 5:34 calls Gamaliel 'a Pharisee' and 'a doctor of the law.' Nicodemus was also a Pharisee, but later clues rule him out."}, {"clue": "I was had in reputation among all the people.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 5:34 uses this exact wording of Gamaliel."}, {"clue": "I told the council to let the apostles alone.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In Acts 5:38 Gamaliel says 'Refrain from these men, and let them alone.' He addresses the council (cf. 5:34-35)."}, {"clue": "Paul was brought up at my feet.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In Acts 22:3 Paul says he was 'brought up in this city at the feet of Gamaliel.' The speaker is Paul (Acts 21:40)."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-gamaliel"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a centurion of the Italian band.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:1 calls Cornelius 'a centurion of the band called the Italian band.' Julius was of 'Augustus' band' (Acts 27:1), so this clue does not fit him."}, {"clue": "I was a devout man who prayed to God alway.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:2 says 'A devout man... and prayed to God alway.'"}, {"clue": "An angel told me to send to Joppa for Peter.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 10:3-5: an angel of God said, 'send men to Joppa, and call for one Simon, whose surname is Peter.'"}, {"clue": "The Holy Ghost fell on all who heard Peter speak in my house.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 10:44 confirms the Holy Ghost fell on all who heard the word. The detail 'in my house' is not in the cited verse; it comes from context (Acts 10:24-27; 11:12). This is a minor addition, accurate to the narrative."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-cornelius"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My husband Aquila and I were tentmakers.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 18:3 says 'by their occupation they were tentmakers,' referring to Aquila and Priscilla (named in v.2), with whom Paul shared the craft."}, {"clue": "We came from Italy when Claudius commanded the Jews to leave Rome.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 18:2 says Aquila was 'lately come from Italy, with his wife Priscilla; (because that Claudius had commanded all Jews to depart from Rome:)'."}, {"clue": "Paul stayed and worked with us in Corinth.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 18:1-3 says Paul came to Corinth, found Aquila and Priscilla, 'abode with them, and wrought.'"}, {"clue": "We expounded the way of God more perfectly to Apollos.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 18:26 says Aquila and Priscilla 'expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-priscilla"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a Jew born at Alexandria.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 18:24 says 'a certain Jew named Apollos, born at Alexandria.'"}, {"clue": "I was an eloquent man, mighty in the scriptures.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 18:24 calls Apollos 'an eloquent man, and mighty in the scriptures.'"}, {"clue": "Aquila and Priscilla taught me the way of God more perfectly.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 18:26 says Aquila and Priscilla 'expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly.' 'Taught' is a fair paraphrase of 'expounded.'"}, {"clue": "Paul wrote that he planted and I watered.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Corinthians 3:6 reads 'I have planted, Apollos watered; but God gave the increase.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-apollos"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was a chief man among the brethren.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 15:22 names Judas Barsabas and Silas as 'chief men among the brethren.'"}, {"clue": "I travelled with Paul after he parted from Barnabas.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 15:40 says Paul chose Silas and departed. The parting from Barnabas is in 15:39, which is not shown, though 15:40 clearly follows it in context."}, {"clue": "I was beaten and put in the stocks with Paul at Philippi.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "Acts 16:22-24 describes the beating and stocks but says only 'them.' Silas is named in 16:19 and 16:25, and Philippi in 16:12; none of these verses is shown. The event itself is accurate."}, {"clue": "At midnight Paul and I prayed and sang praises.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Acts 16:25 explicitly names Paul and Silas praying and singing praises at midnight."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-silas"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was the wife of Heber the Kenite.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 4:17 explicitly names 'Jael the wife of Heber the Kenite.'"}, {"clue": "Sisera fled to my tent.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 4:17 states Sisera 'fled away on his feet to the tent of Jael.'"}, {"clue": "I gave him milk when he asked for water.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 4:19: he asked for 'a little water,' and she 'opened a bottle of milk, and gave him drink.'"}, {"clue": "I drove a nail of the tent into his temples.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 4:21: Jael 'took a nail of the tent... and smote the nail into his temples.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-jael"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I was king in Judaea when Jesus was born.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 2:1 places Jesus' birth in Bethlehem of Judaea 'in the days of Herod the king.'"}, {"clue": "I was troubled when wise men asked for the King of the Jews.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 2:2-3 records the wise men's question and says 'Herod the king... was troubled.'"}, {"clue": "I told the wise men to bring me word again.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 2:8 has him say 'bring me word again.' The 'he' refers back to Herod in 2:7."}, {"clue": "I ordered the children in Bethlehem slain.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Matthew 2:16 says Herod 'sent forth, and slew all the children that were in Bethlehem.' 'Ordered' fairly paraphrases 'sent forth.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-herod"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "My husband Nabal was churlish.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 25:3 names Nabal as Abigail's husband and says he was 'churlish and evil in his doings.'"}, {"clue": "I brought bread, wine and figs to David's men.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "1 Samuel 25:18 lists loaves, bottles of wine and cakes of figs that Abigail prepared. The verse does not name who received them, but the context and 25:27 show they were for David's young men, so this is acceptable paraphrase."}, {"clue": "I persuaded David not to shed blood.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "In 1 Samuel 25:33 David blesses Abigail's advice, which 'hast kept me this day from coming to shed blood.'"}, {"clue": "After Nabal died, I became David's wife.", "supported": "partly", "reason": "1 Samuel 25:42 confirms she 'became his wife,' but Nabal's death is recorded in 25:38-39, not in the cited verse. The clue is accurate overall but adds detail not in the displayed text."}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-abigail"}
{"clue_checks": [{"clue": "I lived in the valley of Sorek.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 16:4 calls Delilah 'a woman in the valley of Sorek.' Saying she lived there is a reasonable paraphrase."}, {"clue": "The lords of the Philistines offered me silver.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 16:5 has the lords of the Philistines promise her 'every one of us eleven hundred pieces of silver.'"}, {"clue": "I asked a strong man the secret of his strength.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 16:6 has Delilah ask Samson 'wherein thy great strength lieth.'"}, {"clue": "I had his seven locks shaved while he slept.", "supported": "yes", "reason": "Judges 16:19 says she 'made him sleep upon her knees' and 'caused him to shave off the seven locks of his head.'"}], "verdict": "pass", "suggested_fix": "", "id": "who-delilah"}
